# What never needs to reach the Docker daemon.
#
# Every image here builds from the repository root, because the Rust build needs `/VERSION` and the
# whole cargo workspace. Without this file that context is several gigabytes of build output, which
# is slow locally and slower in CI — and the cargo `target/` in particular would be copied in and
# then shadowed by the container's own target directory, so it is pure waste.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE EXCLUDES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY NO LONGER DOES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# It excludes build output, dependency trees and runtime scratch. It does NOT try to exclude source
# — not `docs/`, not `scripts/`, not the root markdown — and that is a correction rather than an
# oversight.
#
# It used to. The rule was "everything the services do not build from", with a growing allowlist of
# narrow `!` re-inclusions underneath it for `docker/web/Dockerfile`. That worked while
# `scripts/build-site.sh` was a placeholder with no inputs. The real one READS THE REPOSITORY —
# which is the entire point of the site: the roadmap table, the ten limitation registers, the
# launcher's palette and nine mirrored documents are generated from their sources rather than
# retyped, so a published page cannot disagree with the repository it describes.
#
# Against that, an allowlist cannot be finished. Reconstructing it empirically took five rounds and
# never converged: the generators open `docs/`, `COMPATIBILITY.md`, two workspace packages, four
# `scripts/lib` helpers and `scripts/services.py`; the mirror then resolves every relative link
# inside those nine documents and fails closed on one that points at a file the context does not
# have; and the site's own test suite reads `README.md` and `.github/workflows/build.yml` on top of
# that. The set is bounded by "whatever a site generator or a site test opens", which is not a set
# anybody can maintain by hand — and each miss is a build that fails deep inside a generator with a
# message about a document, not about a Dockerfile.
#
# The exclusions it gave up are worth 3.5 MB. `docs/` is 2.5 MB, `scripts/` 344 KB, `fixtures/`
# 604 KB, the root markdown 43 KB. That is the whole saving the allowlist was defending, and it was
# never the reason this file exists — `target/` and `build/` are.

# Build output
target/
build/
library/java/*/build/
library/java/*/bin/
library/java/build-logic/build/
.gradle/
node_modules/
app/gen/

# Dependency trees and build output inside the bun workspace packages. `node_modules/` above is
# anchored at the context root, so it matches none of these. `dist/` is a build output twice over:
# the web image produces its own inside the builder, and copying a laptop's in would only shadow it
# for one layer.
web/node_modules/
web/dist/
library/ts/nodera-ui/node_modules/
app/ui/node_modules/
app/ui/dist/

# Tooling
.idea/
.vscode/

# Runtime artifacts (see .gitignore for why these exist)
/run/
/nodera/
/logs/
/world-store/
/divergences/
.jqwik-database

# --- why `.git/` is NOT excluded ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# It was, and it is the one exclusion whose removal is load-bearing rather than incidental.
#
# The site mirrors nine documents. Eight are files on disk; the ninth is `services:README.md`, which
# lives on the ORPHAN `services` branch and has no working-tree path at all. `mirror-docs.mjs` reads
# it with `git show` and FAILS CLOSED when the ref is absent, because a mirror that quietly drops a
# document is worse than one that stops. The same history dates every mirrored page from
# `git log -1`; without it every "last updated" on the site silently becomes the day the image was
# built. `build.yml` already established the recipe this depends on — `fetch-depth: 0` plus
# `git fetch origin services:services badges:badges` — and `containers.yml` now carries it too.
#
# The cost is stated rather than hidden: ~28 MB of history, in a shared context, so the three Rust
# images pay it as well. It is not the "several gigabytes" the header above was written against, and
# a second web-only ignore file would be a second place to state these rules — which is the defect
# this repository keeps finding in other shapes.
